# 5. Measurement framework

## Direct service measures

These are most directly influenced by the program:

- First-contact resolution;
- Customer effort score;
- Time to meaningful response;
- Time to resolution;
- Repeat-contact rate;
- Transfer rate;
- Complaint reopening rate;
- Callback completion;
- Refund or replacement completion time;
- Accuracy and consistency of answers;
- Proactive notification delivery and engagement.

## Business outcomes

These should be monitored but interpreted alongside other business factors:

- Repurchase rate;
- Retention;
- Customer lifetime value;
- Loyalty-program engagement;
- Margin after recovery costs;
- Complaints to regulators or public channels.

Where possible, compare pilot customers with a similar control group. If a control group is not feasible, use pre- and post-pilot comparisons while accounting for promotions, peak periods, delivery changes, and product availability.

Suggested pilot thresholds might include:

- 15% reduction in repeat contacts for the selected issue;
- 10% improvement in customer effort;
- 10% improvement in first-contact resolution;
- Faster resolution without a material increase in compensation cost;
- No deterioration in AI accuracy or performance for local-language users;
- Improved repurchase among affected customers relative to the control group.

Final targets should be adjusted after baseline performance is established.

---

# 6. Phased implementation

## First 0–30 days: quick wins

- Confirm the top customer problem;
- Establish baseline metrics;
- Review local language, channel, and regulatory requirements;
- Rewrite the highest-volume support content;
- Create clearer escalation rules;
- Introduce callback options where waiting times are high;
- Give agents limited, documented recovery authority;
- Create a daily review of urgent unresolved cases.

These actions require relatively little technology investment and can improve consistency quickly.

## Days 30–90: focused pilot

Pilot the late- or failed-delivery journey for one geography, customer segment, product category, or delivery partner:

- Risk-based delivery notifications;
- Local-language communication;
- Customer choice of revised delivery, refund, or replacement;
- Case ownership for unresolved failures;
- Unified case notes, even if full system integration is not yet available;
- Agent recovery authority;
- Follow-up after complex cases.

A manual or semi-automated pilot may be preferable initially. This allows the company to learn before investing in predictive logistics or a complete service-platform integration.

## After 90 days: scale proven improvements

If the pilot meets its targets:

- Integrate case history with order, payment, and logistics systems;
- Expand proactive notifications to other service failures;
- Add carefully governed AI assistance;
- Extend local-language staffing and content;
- Formalize the root-cause review process;
- Scale to additional geographies or customer segments.

Integration-heavy initiatives should not be treated as prerequisites for the first test.

